  },
  {
    "episode_id": 46776,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/6/46776.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 20,
    "turn_text": "Oh, being in the office without question. I mean, when you're running for the office, You know, your focus is specifically the voters at home and really the subset of voters who typically vote in primaries. Your message and the issues you talk about are pretty focused. You're talking obviously for our area of water quality, you're talking about tax policy, you're talking about the Constitution, immigration a little bit, so on and so forth. Up there, it's 100 miles a minute. You're having a deal with issues from all over the country, frankly, all over the world. At a moment's notice, some new issue drops in your lab. You have to quickly figure out what that is. The one part of politics that people don't really like, and I'll tell you, even the members don't like it, you're still fundraising. You're doing that all the time. And so, you know, it's definitely the job is much harder than the campaigns. Why are you still fundraising? Well, first part is that because we never closed campaign accounts at the federal level. So right now I'm fundraising because you just don't want to create a race you don't need to have. The one thing that has happened to members in the past is they won the election. They didn't fundraise and they woke up the next election year and somebody was like, well, I want to run. And now you have a fight on your hands and you have to go back and raise all the money. So you like perpetually",
