    "turn_text": "Well, I mean, look, one of the tough things is, especially growing up in inner city, is that we knew we were going to encounter NYPD. It was just a matter of time. Like, even if you're just walking down the street, they're going to pull up and be like, hey, where are you going? That happened to me when I was a kid. It has happened. At the same time. The one thing I want to stress for a lot of young kids in the black community is that if you look at the data, the data is actually pretty clear that we do engage law enforcement, black men at a higher rate than any other subsection of the country. In part, that's because young black men also commit, unfortunately, more average, more often than not, most of the violent crime in the United States, specifically in urban communities. So if we as a black man wanna say we don't wanna feel that the police are looking at us, then as black men in totality, we gotta start deciding how are we gonna comport ourselves? It's tough to say that.",
