  {
    "episode_id": 46776,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/6/46776.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 213,
    "turn_text": "But we have to be honest with ourselves. The vast majority of police, when they engage people in my community, the interactions are fine. The interactions are actually done. A lot of times, people in my community, we want the police there. We want the police, because let's be very clear. The vast majority of crime that happens against black people is committed by black people. That's tough to say, but that's the truth. Don't believe me? Go ask people in Dunbar right now. Who's in Dunbar committing crime against black people? Ask them. Seriously. Well, how do we, how does that, how does that change? I think that's a culture change, you know, of, of, in the black community. I think you're going to start seeing it shifting out as we talk right now. The one key stat that I feel for, you know, as a black man in America, for the black community, which is incredibly important, is the formation of black families are on the rise again. You gotta have father figures in these homes. I think black attainment in education is on the rise in the United States. That's a quality thing. I think two family homes in the black communities on the rise. That's a wonderful thing. And we're starting to see for the first time in 60 years, 67 years in the United States, the rise of the black middle class again. It's happening right in front of our eyes. I went to college with a lot of them. with a lot of people. You know, my colleagues, where they went and got a college degree, it may have worked out, it may have not. They figured out a way to be successful in their lives, their black middle class families raising their kids the right way. And I think that is all that stuff is helpful with respect to how the black community deals with law enforcement as we go forward. But I think it's important, as we talk about this, we got to understand. The vast majority of occurrences with police in the black community are actually positive. The sad part is when we see these flashpoints and these flashpoints are just magnified in front of our eyes.",
