    "turn_text": "it's it's bad it's bad policy i was actually in the senate testifying in front of a little is with warrants committee on this uh... tuesday two zero wednesday uh... in my statements were very clear is that you know there's a couple things number one who's going to pay it or college is going to go pay the money back Well, if colleges pay the money back, then you can forget about people in college right now actually getting an education or the people after them. So that's number one. Is it going to be the people who didn't go to college? Because frankly, that's what will happen. It'll be a forgiveness from the treasury. The treasury is going to have to pay that debt, which means people who didn't go to college are going to have to pay that debt, which means people who are actively paying their student loans right now are going to be making their payments and they got to pay that debt. I just think it's the wrong policy. If we should be doing anything, we got to look at college affordability. And the last part that we should be looking at is looking at a calculation of how much the federal government will lend depending on what you study. Listen, a humanities degree does not have the same value as an accounting degree. It just doesn't. It may have a value to you personally, but it doesn't have an economic value in our economy. And so we have to take a hard look at ourselves and say, why would we give you unlimited dollars to get a piece of paper that doesn't have economic value?",
