  {
    "episode_id": 46776,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/6/46776.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 225,
    "turn_text": "well number one not everybody's prepared to go do it number two not everybody wants to go study in a field that's economically viable You know, if you were struggling in high school and then you went to college and you don't know what you're doing, why are you in college? I tell young kids now, when I talk to young kids who are about to graduate, I ask them, do you know what you want to do? They're like, well, no. I said, then don't go. And the parents look at me like, whoa, what are you talking about? And I'm telling the parents, you do not want to take on debt just to be in college to have the experience because that's going to come to an end. And then you're going to have to spend your life paying that money back. That is illogical. If you don't know what you want to do, go to your little community college, go to Florida, Southwestern State College, take a couple classes, get a job and figure out your life and figure out your focus. And then at that point, if you want to go to four year college university, they're not going anywhere. If you're smart enough, they'll take you. You can find a way. We have this",
