    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/6/46776.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 265,
    "turn_text": "The way you do that is a couple of things. Number one is about provide a lot of times when you deal with access issues is because it's not a providers in the space. So there's a scarcity of the provider. And if there's a scarcity of the provider, then the cost of the provider's time now goes up. So what we have to look at is more slots in residencies and the life of people that will go and achieve that. The second thing we got to do is do more identification early on. A lot of this stuff, and if you've ever really sat down and gone through counseling sessions and actually sat down with people who do this for a living, A lot of it stems from childhood. There is a trigger, the trigger point in childhood that then lays out the decisions that we do as adults. And so a lot of this is trying to assess, and you know what, this is not something you really want schools to be doing. Schools are the easiest state in place to do it because of the access point for most people at an early age. But you do want to make sure the apparatus exists and we're going to frankly have to build one. and I don't have all the answers. So",
