    "turn_idx": 324,
    "turn_text": "Well, I will tell you that phosphorus levels in the south part of the lake where a lot of it comes out of is significantly lower than in the northern part of the lake. The northern part of the lake, the phosphorus content is prohibitively higher. So the lake itself has some natural cleaning mechanisms with its seagrass and so on and so forth, and frankly just sunlight. So there are natural cleaning metrics in the lake itself. So, but the key thing is, and this is important, this is why I say controlling water north of the lake helps control the flow coming out of the lake. Because if we can control flow, it helps with the quality issues about how much water we're taking in. The other parts are, and this is also north of the lake, repairing the Kissimmee river basin to actually have more natural filtration will actually bring cleaner fresh water into the lake. So that one is dispersed out. It is fine. One of the issues we have is when we have, you know, fresh water interacting with salt water, you know, yeah, that issue here, we do need some of it. The problem is how much the course sends our way. Well, Congressman Asinai, we really had our first conversation about that a couple of days ago. We're going to go through a more comprehensive schedule, and it's not just the two of us. It's going to be Congressman Stu, be Congressman Diaz-Ballard, Congresswoman Washington Schultz, and the like. We're all going to Congresswoman. Oh gosh, I forgot her name. She's in Palm Beach. Uh, Frankel and then I'm like, we're going to be all sitting down and going through this because it affects our",
